ame id as before), it's skipped; otherwise, added in order of first occurrence.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So the code would look like:\n\ndef dedupe_records(records):\n    seen_ids = set()\n    result = []\n    for record in records:\n        if 'id' not in record:\n            result.append(record)\n        else:\n            current_id = record['id']\n            if current_id not in seen_ids:\n                seen_ids.add(current_id)\n                result.append(record)\n    return result\n\nTesting this with some examples. Let's say:\n\nExample 1: records = [{'a':1}, {'b':2}, {'c':3}, {'d':4, 'e':5}, {'d':4}]\n\nThe output should be the first four entries because the last one has id d which was already seen.\n\nAnother example where some have no id:\n\nrecords = [{'id': 1}, {'a':2}, {'b':3}, {'c':4}]\nThe result would include all except if there are duplicates. But since each has unique ids, they all stay. The ones without 'id' (if any) would be added as is.\n\nWhat about a record that doesn't have an id but another with the same id? For example:\n\nrecords = [{'a':1}, {'b':2}, {'c':3}, {'d':4}, {'e':5, 'f':6}]\nNo duplicates here. All are kept.\n\nAnother case: records = [{'id': 10}, {'id': 10}]. The first is added to result and the set. The second has id already in the set, so it's skipped. So output is [{'id':10}].\n\nThis seems correct.\n\nNow, what about if a record has multiple keys but no 'id'? Like {'x':1}, then it's added as is.\n\nYes, that's handled by checking if 'id' is not present before adding to the result.\n\nSo this code should meet all the requirements. It doesn't mutate any input lists (since we're creating new ones), and it preserves order correctly.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef dedupe_records(records):\n    seen_ids = set()\n    result = []\n    for record in records:\n        if 'id' not in record:\n            result.append(record)\n        else:\n            current_id = record['id']\n            if current_id not in seen_ids:\n        